}","title":"Kotlin/Wasm JavaScript interoperability","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/wasm-js-interop.html","sections":["Interoperability with JavaScript > Use JavaScript code in Kotlin > JavaScript modules"],"line_start":180,"line_end":206,"score":0.6602146625518799,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"functions-with-the-jsexport-annotation","start":7678,"end":8985,"split_code":false,"platforms":["wasm"],"id":"91bc13dcaa244d70564646505fd6214018ea3b7669fc7cc867efdf7f4cdf8be9","document_id":"wasm-js-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"Functions with the @JsExport annotation

To make a Kotlin/Wasm function available to JavaScript code, use the @JsExport annotation:

// Kotlin/Wasm

@JsExport
fun addOne(x: Int): Int = x + 1

Kotlin/Wasm functions marked with the @JsExport annotation are visible as properties on a default export of the generated .mjs module. You can then use this function in JavaScript:

// JavaScript

import exports from "./module.mjs"

exports.addOne(10)

The Kotlin/Wasm compiler is capable of generating TypeScript definitions from any @JsExport declarations in your Kotlin code. These definitions can be used by IDEs and JavaScript tools to provide code autocompletion, help with type-checks, and make it easier to consume Kotlin code from JavaScript and TypeScript.

The Kotlin/Wasm compiler collects any top-level functions marked with the @JsExport annotation and automatically generates TypeScript definitions in a .d.ts file.

To generate TypeScript definitions, in your build.gradle.kts file in the wasmJs{} block, add the generateTypeScriptDefinitions() function:

kotlin {
    wasmJs {
        binaries.executable()
        browser {
        }
        generateTypeScriptDefinitions()
    }
}

Generating TypeScript declaration files in Kotlin/Wasm is Experimental . It may be dropped or changed at any time.","title":"Kotlin/Wasm JavaScript interoperability","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/wasm-js-interop.html","sections":["Interoperability with JavaScript > Use Kotlin code in JavaScript > Functions with the @JsExport annotation"],"line_start":246,"line_end":278,"score":0.6582968235015869,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"export-value-classes","start":4801,"end":5522,"split_code":false,"platforms":["js"],"id":"f60ee257bc68fbb1db0cc9c8e023954bbaf0c921c300e62db8d26acce57e3575","document_id":"js-to-kotlin-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"Export value classes

You can export Kotlin's inline value classes as regular TypeScript classes.

To export a value class, mark it with the @JsExport annotation on the Kotlin side:

// Kotlin
@JsExport
@JvmInline
