}

All declarations inside the external class are implicitly considered external.","title":"Kotlin/Wasm JavaScript interoperability","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/wasm-js-interop.html","sections":["Interoperability with JavaScript > Use JavaScript code in Kotlin > External declarations > JavaScript classes"],"line_start":47,"line_end":70,"score":0.6311917901039124,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"jsreference-type","start":10024,"end":10778,"split_code":false,"platforms":["wasm"],"id":"aa381dd3cc752d38578a160ba773fb387e1bdfc841efcd9fed0d8c7cec9ae998","document_id":"wasm-js-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"JsReference type

Kotlin values can be passed to JavaScript as opaque references using the JsReference type.

For example, if you want to expose this Kotlin class User to JavaScript:

class User(var name: String)

You can use the toJsReference() function to create JsReference<User> and return it to JavaScript:

@JsExport
fun createUser(name: String): JsReference<User> {
    return User(name).toJsReference()
}

These references aren't directly available in JavaScript and behave like empty frozen JavaScript objects. To operate on these objects, you need to export more functions to JavaScript using the get() method where you unwrap the reference value:

@JsExport
fun setUserName(user: JsReference<User>, name: String) {
    user.get().name = name
}","title":"Kotlin/Wasm JavaScript interoperability","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/wasm-js-interop.html","sections":["Interoperability with JavaScript > Type correspondence > JsReference type"],"line_start":318,"line_end":338,"score":0.6281245946884155,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"use-long-in-exported-declarations","start":9908,"end":10498,"split_code":false,"platforms":["js"],"id":"2acd20adaf8e5b999755359c377e388646b2d7453c4c2be926528825ccd52fc3","document_id":"js-to-kotlin-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"Use Long in exported declarations

Since Kotlin's Long type can compile to JavaScript's BigInt type, Kotlin/JS supports exporting Long values to JavaScript.

To enable this feature:

Allow exporting Long in Kotlin/JS. Add the following compiler option to the freeCompilerArgs attribute in your build.gradle(.kts) file:

// build.gradle.kts
kotlin {
   js {
       ...
       compilerOptions { 
           freeCompilerArgs.add("-XXLanguage:+JsAllowLongInExportedDeclarations")
       }
   }
}

Enable the BigInt type. See how to enable it in Use BigInt type to represent Kotlin's Long type .","title":"Use Kotlin code from JavaScript","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/js-to-kotlin-interop.html","sections":["Use Kotlin code from JavaScript > Package structure > Use BigInt type to represent Kotlin's Long type > Use Long in exported declarations"],"line_start":264,"line_end":282,"score":0.627407431602478,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"}]}

